  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nThursday played out fairly close to expectations for most of the \narea. with the main set-up through the middle of the forecast area \nmuch of the afternoon and eventually pushing toward the coast. \nWidespread rainfall totals over an inch occurred from Hebbronville to \nPleasanton and eastward with isolated totals over 4 inches. \nConvection continues along the coast at the time of writing, but \nwe're also watching more moving out of Deep South Texas that will \naffect inland areas later in the night. This is aligning well with \nmid-level vorticity stretched out from the coast and more diffuse \nbut spread out over areas to the south.  As the mid-level low gets \nstretched tonight and tomorrow expect a general north and west \nprogression in the highest rainfall chances, though with moisture \ncontinuing to push in from the Gulf we will not end rain chances \neast either. In fact, eventually, a convergence boundary will set up \nalong the coast and maintain rain chances well into next week, but \nmore on that shortly. \n\nWith more diffuse forcing, while we'll keep the rain chances, the \nstrength of the convection should not be what we saw today. PWAT \nvalues do remain elevated though so heavy downpours can still be \nexpected.  Those PWAT values, by the way, hit 2.5 on the evening \nsounding, which crushes the old (anomalously low) record for the day \nof 2.09. This may have been slightly contaminated by nearby showers \nat launch time, but is reasonable to forecast values, so probably \nisn't too far off. Moisture remains elevated into next week despite \na weak cold front that should sink into the area on Saturday. \nInstead of drying us out, this could set up an isentropic lift \npattern (it's been a while) that will just help keep those rain \nchances in place especially along the coast. Bottom line, don't \nexpect to see much of the sun for the next week, and generally light \nrainfall daily through at least Tuesday.  But on the bright side, \nhigh temps remain at or slightly below normal through mid-week as \nwell.",
